{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATOR DECISIONS (2026-09-02) — both items from the preceding comment are APPROVED. Change 2551 stands as pushed; no amendment made.\n\n1. **Firing on 2 datapoints: APPROVED.** The review_interrupts_bound_exceeded and review_interrupts_signal_unavailable alarms keep the 900/4/2 shape. The operator accepts the detection-latency trade: ~15 min -> ~30 min on the signal-unavailable alarm, in exchange for immunity to flapping on ordinary probe-timer jitter under treat_missing_data=breaching. The pre-bff5 1-of-1 latch is deliberately retired — it is incompatible with breaching semantics, because a single absent period would page. The mitigating property stands on the record: the failure this alarm watches recurs on EVERY deploy while broken, so it accumulates 2 datapoints quickly in a real fault.\n\n2. **Scope growth: APPROVED for this change.** The gerrit_data_disk_high widening (monitoring.tf, 300/1/none -> 300/3/2) stays in 2551 even though that alarm was not on this ticket's flip list. It was already breaching from the earlier partial fix on 3276 but at evaluation_periods=1 with no datapoints_to_alarm, which is a live flap hazard; the new widening guard catches it, and reverting the hunk would require relaxing that guard. Keeping it means the guard stays strict.\n\nBoth decisions were escalated before the fact rather than absorbed, and are recorded here so the reasoning survives this session."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788402206123457001,"uuid":"bf6c10dc-ccd0-484b-b23b-804b83fe7707"}